# minimum Qore version
%requires qore >= 0.9.4

%modern

module DatasourceProvider {
    version = "1.2";
    desc    = "API for providing datasource connection strings";
    author  = "David Nichols <david.nichols@qoretechnologies.com>";
    url     = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage DatasourceProvider Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section datasourceproviderintro DatasourceProvider Module Introduction

    The %DatasourceProvider module provides an API for providing @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource "Datasource"
    connection strings.

    To use this module, use \c "%requires DatasourceProvider" in your code.

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the DatasourceProvider namespace

    Functions:
    - @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_string() "get_ds_string()"
    - @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_hash() "get_ds_hash()"

    @subsection datasourceprovider_modules Datasource Provider Modules

    This module uses the \c "QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS" environment variable to load datasource provider
    modules.  Each datasource provider module must provide two public functions; one called
    \c get_mod_ds_string() with the following signature:
    @code{.py}
public string sub get_mod_ds_string(string str) {}
    @endcode

    And one called \c get_mod_ds_hash() returning a hash keyed by datasource name where values are datasource strings
    with the following signature:
    @code{.py}
public *hash<string, string> sub get_mod_ds_hash() {}
    @endcode

    For \c get_mod_ds_string(), if the string argument is not a valid identifier for a datasource, the function should
    throw an exception (any exception; the exception is not displayed by default).

    @subsubsection datasourceprovider_caller_scoped Datasource Provider Modules and Restricted Callers

    The datasources of a datasource provider module belong to the host that configures the module, so code that may
    not receive the host's secrets (see
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::callerMayReceiveHostSecrets() "SandboxManager::callerMayReceiveHostSecrets()": code that
    a sandbox governs, or code restricted from external information with
    @ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_INFO "PO_NO_EXTERNAL_INFO") gets no datasource from it by
    @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_string() "get_ds_string()" or @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_hash() "get_ds_hash()".

    A datasource provider module that resolves names for the calling code itself, and returns to every caller only
    the datasources that the caller may use and read (its own, for example), declares it with a public function named
    as given by @ref DatasourceProvider::CallerScopedProviderFunction "CallerScopedProviderFunction" that returns
    @ref True "True", as in this example:
    @code{.py}
# declares that every datasource that the module returns is one that the calling code may use and read
public bool sub get_mod_caller_scoped() {
    return True;
}
    @endcode

    Only such a module is used for code that may not receive the host's secrets; its datasource strings are returned
    to that code as to any other code, with their passwords.  Trusted code can use the datasources of every module for
    restricted code with a policy barrier
    (@ref Qore::SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() "SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy()") or a capability
    (@ref Qore::SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall() "SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall()").

    Datasource provider modules declared in the \c "QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS" environment variable must
    be separated by the platform-specific @ref Qore::PathSep "PathSep" character as in the following
    examples:

    @par Unix Example
    @verbatim
export QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS=MyDatasourceProvider:OtherDatasourceProvider
    @endverbatim

    @par Windows CMD.EXE Example
    @verbatim
set QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS=MyDatasourceProvider;OtherDatasourceProvider
    @endverbatim

    @par Windows PowerShell Example
    @verbatim
$env:QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS="MyDatasourceProvider;OtherDatasourceProvider"
    @endverbatim

    @section datasourceproviderrelnotes Release Notes

    @subsection datasourceprovider_1_2 DatasourceProvider 1.2
    - code that may not receive the host's secrets (code that a sandbox governs, or code restricted from external
      information) gets the datasources of only the datasource provider modules that resolve names for the calling
      code themselves (see @ref datasourceprovider_caller_scoped): a lookup of any other datasource fails as for an
      unknown identifier, and @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_hash() "get_ds_hash()" leaves such datasources out
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/6844">issue 6844</a>)
    - can be loaded first by code without terminal I/O (@ref Qore::PO_NO_TERMINAL_IO "PO_NO_TERMINAL_IO"); the verbose
      output of @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_string() "get_ds_string()" and
      @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_hash() "get_ds_hash()" is left out when the module's code or the code that calls
      it has no terminal I/O (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/6438">issue 6438</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/7140">issue 7140</a>)
    - the \c DATASOURCE-ERROR exceptions and the script path of the program of a provider module show the masked
      description (see @ref Qore::SQL::mask_datasource(string) "mask_datasource()") of a string given that is not a
      plain datasource identifier, such as a datasource string, which can have a user name, a password and a database
      name, instead of the string; the verbose output of @ref DatasourceProvider::get_ds_string() "get_ds_string()"
      shows no text of the exception of a provider module for such a string
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/6988">issue 6988</a>)

    @subsection datasourceprovider_1_1 DatasourceProvider 1.1
    - added support for the \c get_ds_hash() function

    @subsection datasourceprovider_1_0 DatasourceProvider 1.0
    - initial release
*/

#! The DatasourceProvider namespace contains all the objects in the DatasourceProvider module
public namespace DatasourceProvider {
    #! The name of the function that declares that a datasource provider module resolves names for the calling code
    /** A datasource provider module that returns to every caller only the datasources that the caller may use and
        read declares it with a public function of this name that takes no argument and returns @ref True; only such
        a module is used for code that may not receive the host's secrets (see @ref datasourceprovider_caller_scoped)

        @since DatasourceProvider 1.2
    */
    public const CallerScopedProviderFunction = "get_mod_caller_scoped";

    #! Returns a datasource connection string
    /** @par Example:
        @code{.py}
string connstr = get_ds_string(str);
        @endcode

        @param str the identifier or datasource connection string
        @param verbose display datasource provider module errors on stdout (otherwise errors are ignored); nothing is
            displayed when the module's code or the code that calls it has no terminal I/O
            (@ref Qore::PO_NO_TERMINAL_IO "PO_NO_TERMINAL_IO", see
            @ref Qore::SandboxManager::isTerminalIoAllowed() "SandboxManager::isTerminalIoAllowed()"); for a string
            given that is not a plain datasource identifier, such as a datasource string, only the error code and the
            masked description of the string (see @ref Qore::SQL::mask_datasource(string) "mask_datasource()") are
            shown, not the text of the error, which can show the string

        @return the datasource connection string

        @throw DATASOURCE-ERROR the connection string cannot be matched as an external datasource identifier by a datasource provider module and there is no driver specification in the string (ex: \c "driver:...")

        @note
        - datasource provider modules are processed in the order they are declared with the \c QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS environment variable; datasource provider modules must be separated with @ref Qore::PathSep "PathSep" characters
        - no database connection is attempted with this function call
        - code that may not receive the host's secrets (see
          @ref Qore::SandboxManager::callerMayReceiveHostSecrets() "SandboxManager::callerMayReceiveHostSecrets()")
          gets a datasource only from a datasource provider module that resolves names for the calling code (see
          @ref datasourceprovider_caller_scoped); for any other identifier, \c DATASOURCE-ERROR is raised as for an
          unknown identifier, without the modules; a datasource string with a driver is returned as it is

        @see @ref datasourceprovider_modules
     */
    public string sub get_ds_string(string str, *bool verbose) {
        # check for a driver
        bool driver = (str =~ /^.+:/);
        # decided here, where the code that calls the module is the caller (qore#6844)
        bool host = SandboxManager::callerMayReceiveHostSecrets();

        # process datasource provider modules in declaration order
        if (ENV.QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS) {
            list<string> pl = ENV.QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS.split(PathSep);
            foreach string mod in (pl) {
                try {
                    *string rv = try_get_ds_string(mod, str, host);
                    if (exists rv) {
                        return rv;
                    }
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    # the stdout constant needs terminal I/O only when it is written, so that the module can be
                    # loaded by code without it; such code gets no output (qore#6438)
                    if (verbose && SandboxManager::isTerminalIoAllowed()) {
                        # the text of a provider module's exception can show the datasource string, so the string
                        # is masked in it (issue #6988)
                        stdout.printf("%s: %s\n", ex.err, mask_ds_text(ex.desc, str));
                    }
                    # otherwise ignore exceptions
                }
            }
            if (!driver) {
                if (!host) {
                    throw_unknown_ds(str);
                }
                throw "DATASOURCE-ERROR", sprintf("datasource identifier %y could not be matched to a known datasource; tried the following datasource provider modules: %y", get_ds_desc(str), pl);
            }
        }

        if (!driver) {
            if (!host) {
                throw_unknown_ds(str);
            }
            throw "DATASOURCE-ERROR", sprintf("datasource identifier %y could not be matched to a known datasource; there is no driver specification in the string (ex: \"driver:...\") and no datasource provider modules are set in the QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS environment variable", get_ds_desc(str));
        }
        return str;
    }

    #! Returns all datasource supported with their connection strings prefixed by \c "db://"
    /** @param verbose display datasource provider module errors on stdout (otherwise errors are ignored); nothing is
            displayed when the module's code or the code that calls it has no terminal I/O (see get_ds_string())

        @note code that may not receive the host's secrets gets only the datasources of the datasource provider
        modules that resolve names for the calling code, which it may read (see @ref datasourceprovider_caller_scoped)
    */
    public *hash<string, string> sub get_ds_hash(*bool verbose) {
        # decided here, where the code that calls the module is the caller (qore#6844)
        bool host = SandboxManager::callerMayReceiveHostSecrets();
        hash<string, string> rv;

        # process datasource provider modules in declaration order
        if (ENV.QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS) {
            list<string> pl = ENV.QORE_DATASOURCE_PROVIDERS.split(PathSep);
            foreach string mod in (pl) {
                try {
                    rv += try_get_ds_hash(mod, host);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    # see get_ds_string()
                    if (verbose && SandboxManager::isTerminalIoAllowed()) {
                        stdout.printf("%s: %s\n", ex.err, ex.desc);
                    }
                    # otherwise ignore exceptions
                }
            }
        }

        return rv;
    }
}

# private, non-exported namespace
namespace Priv {
    # returns the string given for messages: a datasource identifier as given, and the masked description of any
    # other string, such as a datasource string, which can have a user name, a password and a database name
    # (issue #6988)
    string sub get_ds_desc(string str) {
        return (str =~ /[\/@:%({]/) ? mask_datasource(str) : str;
    }

    # returns the text of an exception for the verbose output: the text is shown for a datasource identifier; for any
    # other string, such as a datasource string, it is not, as a provider module can show the string or a part of it
    # in any form (issue #6988)
    string sub mask_ds_text(*string text, string str) {
        string desc = get_ds_desc(str);
        return desc == str
            ? (text ?? "")
            : sprintf("(the text of the error is not shown, as it can show the datasource string %s)", desc);
    }

    # the error that code that may not receive the host's secrets gets for a datasource that it may not use: the
    # same as for an unknown identifier, without the host's modules (qore#6844)
    sub throw_unknown_ds(string str) {
        throw "DATASOURCE-ERROR", sprintf("datasource identifier %y could not be matched to a known datasource",
            get_ds_desc(str));
    }

    # returns True if the module loaded in the Program declares that it resolves names for the calling code
    # (qore#6844); fails closed: a module without the declaration, or whose declaration fails or returns anything
    # else, does not
    bool sub is_caller_scoped(Program p) {
        if (!p.existsFunction(DatasourceProvider::CallerScopedProviderFunction)) {
            return False;
        }
        try {
            return p.callFunction(DatasourceProvider::CallerScopedProviderFunction) === True;
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            return False;
        }
    }

    # loads the module in a Program of its own; for code that may not receive the host's secrets, returns the
    # Program only for a module that resolves names for the calling code, and gives no error of the other modules,
    # which are the host's (qore#6844)
    *Program sub load_provider(string mod, string script_path, bool host) {
        Program p();
        if (host) {
            p.loadModule(mod);
            # we cannot make the following call before the Program::loadModule() call or it will break loading
            # modules from relative paths
            p.setScriptPath(script_path);
            return p;
        }
        try {
            p.loadModule(mod);
            p.setScriptPath(script_path);
            if (is_caller_scoped(p)) {
                return p;
            }
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # the error of a module that the calling code may not use is the host's
        }
    }

    # returns the datasource string from the module; for code that may not receive the host's secrets, only from a
    # module that resolves names for the calling code, otherwise NOTHING
    *string sub try_get_ds_string(string mod, string str, bool host) {
        # the script path appears in exception locations (issue #6988)
        *Program p = load_provider(mod, sprintf("Qore::DatasourceProvider(%s,%s)", mod, get_ds_desc(str)), host);
        return p ? p.callFunction("get_mod_ds_string", str) : NOTHING;
    }

    # returns the datasource strings of the module; for code that may not receive the host's secrets, only those of
    # a module that resolves names for the calling code
    *hash<string, string> sub try_get_ds_hash(string mod, bool host) {
        *Program p = load_provider(mod, sprintf("Qore::DatasourceProvider(%s)", mod), host);
        return p ? p.callFunction("get_mod_ds_hash") : NOTHING;
    }
}
